WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.120
You triaged the alert, scoped the blast radius, built the timeline, and made your containment calls.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:12.550
But none of it matters to anyone else until it exists outside your head. The report is the deliverable.

3
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:18.930
Remember who reads it. Your manager, to decide whether to notify the executive team.

4
00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:22.870
The incident-response team, to eradicate the adversary.

5
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.810
The detection engineers, to close the gap that let it happen.

6
00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:32.640
And months later, maybe a regulator, an auditor, or a lawyer. Write for all of them.

7
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:40.080
Five parts. Open with what happened — a plain-language summary a busy manager can read in thirty seconds.

8
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.380
The timeline — normalized times, with the source for every step.

9
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:49.040
Scope and impact — which hosts, accounts, and data were actually touched.

10
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.980
Actions taken — what you contained, and exactly when.

11
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.590
And recommendations — the concrete changes that stop this recurring.

12
00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:02.840
One discipline above all: separate what you observed from what you concluded.

13
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:11.220
State the evidence as fact, and label your interpretation as interpretation. A reader must be able to tell the log line from your theory.

14
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:18.220
So the investigation isn't finished when you understand it — it's finished when someone else can act on it.

15
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:25.230
Summary, timeline, scope, actions, recommendations — write it clearly, and your work finally counts.
